<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">LGT Group</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as LGT Bank</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">LGT is a private banking and asset management group owned by the princely family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, which took over the Bank in Liechtenstein founded in 1920. In 2008 data stolen from its trust subsidiary exposed German tax evaders, causing a diplomatic dispute and international pressure on Liechtenstein's banking secrecy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lgt-group.jpg" alt="LGT Group" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LGT · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LGT_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The bank was founded as the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> sent its currency, then legal tender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>, into decline; the Swiss franc became legal tender there in 1924. The Bank of Liechtenstein, known as BIL, was set up on the initiative of the Princely House together with industrialists and financiers of the former empire. The Anglo-Austrian Bank received the concession from the government on 30 August 1920 and was the majority stakeholder, with Count Karl Trauttmanssdorf as first chairman. BIL opened in May 1921 with 10 employees in the main government building. In 1930 the Princely House acquired more than 90% of its shares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until the 1960s the bank chiefly financed the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>'s economy, then grew into an international commercial bank dealing in mortgages, stock markets and foreign exchange. In 1970 its capital passed to the new Prince of Liechtenstein Foundation. It opened offices abroad in the 1980s, starting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, went public in 1986 and in 1989 acquired GT Management PLC in London. In 1996 the group became Liechtenstein Global Trust; it sold its GT Asset Management division in 1998, went private again and turned to private banking and asset management.</p><p>In February 2008 German tax investigators examined several hundred clients of its former trust subsidiary, LGT Treuhand, from a CD acquired by the Bundesnachrichtendienst. The bank said the data had been stolen in 2002 and named the suspect, Heinrich Kieber. The information went to many other European countries and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. On 16 December 2010 German proceedings against the group were discontinued in return for about €46 million, without an admission of guilt. In 2011 the German supervisor BaFin blocked its purchase of BHF-Bank, and the group withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for some years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The group's ultimate parent is the LGT Group Foundation, which holds the shares for the Princely Family. Since a reorganisation in 2020 and 2021 its units have worked as separate companies: LGT Private Banking, LGT Capital Partners for asset management, and Lightrock for impact investing. It also supports the Princely Collections of European art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “LGT Group” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
